64,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
65,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option C is the only logical choice. A shared calendar solves the issue of disproportionate use, and a formal booking system creates the accountability we need to ensure the room is left clean.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,Option C also provides the privacy we currently lack. Having a dedicated space for discussions would prevent difficult conversations from spilling over into the main office and leaving us in a cold silence.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,May,,"Wait, a shared calendar? That just sounds like another thing for one person to manage while everyone else ignores it. And Option A is a total nightmare—""maintained by whoever uses them"" is just code for ""May does the dishes.""","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"Option C is the only one that actually addresses the 68,000 yen we've been throwing away on outside rentals. It's a direct solution to a financial leak, unlike a kitchen or a nap room.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,1,talk,Servas,,"While Option C addresses the finances, we must consider the thinness of the walls. If it becomes a hub for discussions, the noise will drift directly to our desks, disturbing the peace we all need to work.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Servas, the noise is a concern, but Option B doesn't solve the financial waste, and Option A only encourages more crumbs and bins. A booking system allows us to regulate the room's use and maintain order.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, I understand your concern for peace, but the current silence between us is a different kind of noise. A meeting space allows us to resolve tensions privately, which may actually lead to a more harmonious office.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,May,,"@Servas, if we go with Option B, we're just paying for a room where people hide. But Option C? That's just a fancy way of saying we'll be spending our ""break"" time in meetings. Is that really saving us any time?","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, it's not about spending ""break"" time in meetings; it's about not spending our budget on a room down the street. Option C is the only choice that makes financial sense. The others are just hobbies.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,2,talk,Servas,,"My friends, we must be careful not to trade one form of waste for another. If we create a meeting space without addressing the sound, we simply move the noise from the hallway to our very ears. Option B offers a sanctuary of silence.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Servas, a sanctuary of silence doesn't address the fact that the room is currently a free-for-all. Option C provides a structure. If we have a calendar, we can set rules for noise levels and cleaning that are actually enforceable.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, I appreciate the need for silence, but a room where we only sit alone may further isolate us. Option C gives us a place to finally bridge the gap between us as colleagues, safely and privately.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,May,,"@Ryuji, ""enforceable"" rules usually just mean the person who cares the most ends up doing the policing. But honestly, compared to the disaster of Option A, C is the only one that doesn't sound like a full-time cleaning job.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Servas, your obsession with ""sanctuary"" is charming, but it doesn't pay the invoices. We are literally paying for a room on the main road because we can't have a simple meeting here. Option C is the only rational choice.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,3,talk,Servas,,"Rationality is a sturdy bridge, Shunichi, but it must lead to a destination where we can actually breathe. If we choose Option C, we must commit to a culture of quiet respect, lest our sanctuary become a source of stress.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,"I have considered all perspectives, but the data remains clear. Option C is the only choice that provides a structural solution to the usage imbalance, the financial waste, and the lack of accountability. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I believe that the health of our team depends on our ability to communicate without fear or audience. Option C provides the necessary sanctuary for those difficult but essential conversations. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,May,,"I'm still not thrilled about the ""management"" part, but Option A is a non-starter and B is just a waste of space. C is the only one that doesn't sound like a chore-trap. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,It's almost refreshing to see you all finally agree on something. Option C stops the financial bleeding and turns a useless room into an asset. I'm voting for Option C.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
84,3,4,talk,Servas,,"It is a rare and beautiful thing to see such unity among us. While my heart still whispers for the silence of Option B, I see that the needs of the collective are clear. I shall vote for Option C, trusting in our shared kindness to keep the noise low.","{""words"":50,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
